<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/plant-nutrition-and-fertilisers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plant nutrition is <strong>feeding crops without feeding pollution</strong>: the NPK chemistry
    is settled science (Liebig\'s law of the minimum, 1840), so the craft is delivery — rate,
    timing, placement — and honesty about the curve\'s flat region.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the trio, the 4Rs, the curve, the runoff bill" widget="equationStep" config={fertCfg} />

  <h2>Sources and modern practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Haber–Bosch feeds half of humanity:</strong> synthetic nitrogen (the geochemical lesson\'s numbers) underpins ~50% of global food protein — the discovery with the largest life-count in history (Smil), and its runoff is the same process\'s shadow.</li>
    <li><strong>Organic vs mineral, functionally:</strong> organic sources (manure, compost) feed soil biology and release slowly; mineral fertilisers are immediately available — most good farming uses both, sequenced. The either/or framing is marketing, not agronomy.</li>
    <li><strong>The leaf tells the truth:</strong> tissue testing at key growth stages diagnoses deficiency directly (yellowing old leaves = nitrogen; purple = phosphorus) — plant symptoms are the fastest lab.</li>
    <li><strong>Variable-rate is arriving:</strong> GPS-mapped soil tests + yield maps → fertiliser prescriptions per zone (the precision lesson\'s economics: soil variation within one field can exceed variation between farms).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Liebig (1840); Smil, Enriching the Earth; the 4R nutrient stewardship framework (IPNI).</p>
</TopicPage>
